## BACKGROUND & CONTEXT

Rule-based crawling represents the most common pattern in web scraping applications, where spiders need to systematically discover and extract content following specific navigation patterns. Unlike single-page scrapers, production crawlers must follow links, apply filters, and route discovered content to appropriate parsing logic based on URL patterns. Scrapy's CrawlSpider pioneered the declarative rule-based approach, allowing developers to define crawling rules as configuration rather than imperative code. This pattern is essential for Ghost because it enables rapid spider development for common scenarios: crawling e-commerce categories, archiving documentation, monitoring news sites, or aggregating job listings across pagination. The CrawlSpider pattern separates concerns—rules define what to follow and how to parse, while the framework handles the crawling mechanics. This abstraction eliminates repetitive boilerplate code for link following, allowing developers to focus on data extraction logic. For complex sites with consistent URL structures, CrawlSpider can reduce spider implementation from hundreds of lines to a simple rule configuration.

## SCRAPY ANALYSIS

Scrapy's CrawlSpider extends Spider with rule-based crawling capabilities centered around the Rule class. Key features include: Rules defining link extraction patterns (allow/deny regex), callback methods for parsing, and follow flags controlling recursive crawling; LinkExtractor integration for flexible link discovery with XPath/CSS restrictions; Callback routing based on URL pattern matching; Deny patterns preventing crawling of specific URL patterns; Process_links hooks for URL transformation before request generation; Process_request hooks for request modification before scheduling; and Follow flags controlling whether to follow links from pages matching the rule. The spider starts with start_urls, extracts links using configured extractors, filters based on rule allow/deny patterns, and routes matching URLs to specified callbacks. Rules are evaluated in order, with the first matching rule determining the callback and follow behavior.

## GHOST INTEGRATION

This feature adds CrawlSpider as a new base class alongside the existing Spider implementation. Integration points include: (1) CrawlSpider base class inheriting from Spider with rule-based crawling; (2) Rule class defining extraction patterns, callbacks, and follow behavior; (3) LinkExtractor integration from Phase 1 for flexible link discovery; (4) SpiderConfiguration extended with RuleConfiguration for declarative setup; (5) Scheduler integration for efficient request queue management; (6) DuplicateFilter preventing infinite loops through already-visited URLs; (7) Signal system emitting RuleMatched, LinkExtracted, and RequestGenerated events; (8) Pipeline integration for processing items yielded by rule callbacks. The implementation will leverage existing Ghost infrastructure while adding the declarative rule abstraction layer.

## TECHNICAL REQUIREMENTS

1. Create CrawlSpider base class inheriting from Spider with rule-based crawling
2. Implement Rule class with allow/deny patterns, callback specification, and follow flag
3. Support regex-based URL pattern matching (allow/deny) with compiled regex caching
4. Integrate with ILinkExtractor for flexible link extraction strategies
5. Implement callback routing system invoking spider methods based on rule matches
6. Support process_links callback for URL transformation before request creation
7. Support process_request callback for request modification before scheduling
8. Create RulesEngine for efficient rule matching with early termination
9. Support rule ordering and priority for deterministic matching behavior
10. Implement cb_kwargs passing additional arguments to callbacks
11. Support follow flag controlling recursive crawling from matched pages
12. Integration with existing Spider lifecycle (StartAsync, CloseAsync, signals)
13. Support restrictions (restrict_xpaths, restrict_css) limiting extraction to page regions
14. Thread-safe implementation for concurrent rule evaluation
15. Comprehensive debugging support with rule matching logging

## CONFIGURATION DESIGN

```csharp
public sealed class CrawlSpiderConfiguration : SpiderConfiguration
{
    public List<RuleConfiguration> Rules { get; set; } = new();
    public bool FollowRedirects { get; set; } = true;
    public int MaxCrawlDepth { get; set; } = 0;  // 0 = unlimited
    public bool AllowExternalLinks { get; set; } = false;
    public LinkExtractorConfiguration LinkExtractor { get; set; } = new();
    public bool LogRuleMatches { get; set; } = false;
    public bool LogExtractedLinks { get; set; } = false;
}

public sealed class RuleConfiguration
{
    public required string Name { get; set; }
    public List<string> AllowPatterns { get; set; } = new();  // Regex patterns for URL matching
    public List<string> DenyPatterns { get; set; } = new();   // Regex patterns to exclude
    public string? CallbackMethod { get; set; }  // Spider method name to invoke
    public bool Follow { get; set; } = true;  // Whether to follow links from matching pages
    public int Priority { get; set; } = 0;  // Higher priority rules evaluated first
    public Dictionary<string, object>? CallbackKwargs { get; set; }
    public List<string>? RestrictXpaths { get; set; }  // Only extract links from these regions
    public List<string>? RestrictCssSelectors { get; set; }
    public string? ProcessLinksMethod { get; set; }  // Method for URL transformation
    public string? ProcessRequestMethod { get; set; }  // Method for request modification
    public bool DenyExternalLinks { get; set; } = true;
}

public sealed class Rule
{
    public required string Name { get; set; }
    public IReadOnlyList<Regex> AllowRegexes { get; set; } = new List<Regex>();
    public IReadOnlyList<Regex> DenyRegexes { get; set; } = new List<Regex>();
    public Func<string, bool>? AllowFunc { get; set; }
    public string? CallbackMethod { get; set; }
    public bool Follow { get; set; } = true;
    public int Priority { get; set; }
    public Dictionary<string, object>? CallbackKwargs { get; set; }
}

public interface IRulesEngine
{
    RuleMatchResult? Match(string url);
    void CompileRules(IEnumerable<Rule> rules);
}

public sealed class RuleMatchResult
{
    public required Rule Rule { get; set; }
    public string CallbackMethod { get; set; } = "Parse";
    public bool ShouldFollow { get; set; }
    public Dictionary<string, object> CallbackKwargs { get; set; } = new();
}

public abstract class CrawlSpider : Spider
{
    protected CrawlSpiderConfiguration CrawlConfig { get; }
    protected ILinkExtractor LinkExtractor { get; }
    protected abstract void SetupRules();
    protected virtual List<string> ProcessLinks(List<string> links) => links;
    protected virtual Request ProcessRequest(Request request) => request;
}
```

## IMPLEMENTATION NOTES

- Compile regex patterns once during rule setup for performance
- Implement rule matching with early termination: first matching rule wins
- Support named groups in regex patterns for extracting URL components
- Process_links callback receives list of extracted URLs, returns filtered/modified list
- Process_request callback receives Request, returns modified Request (or null to drop)
- Use reflection to invoke callback methods by name, with fallback to Parse()
- Support async callback methods through reflection detection
- Implement depth tracking via Request.Meta["depth"] to enforce MaxCrawlDepth
- Integrate with DuplicateFilter to prevent revisiting already-seen URLs
- For external link handling, check domain against start_urls domain
- Support multiple start URLs with different domains (multi-site crawling)
- Implement rule composition: rules can inherit from base rules
- Add rule statistics: match count, extraction count, callback timing
- Support conditional rules: rules that only apply based on page content
- Debug logging: log which rule matched, what links were extracted, callback invoked
- Handle circular references: track redirect chains to prevent infinite loops
- Support rule groups for organizing complex crawling logic

## TESTING STRATEGY

- Unit tests: Verify rule matching logic with various allow/deny pattern combinations
- Regex tests: Verify pattern compilation and matching performance
- Callback routing tests: Verify correct spider method invoked based on matching rule
- Process_links tests: Verify URL transformation callback works correctly
- Process_request tests: Verify request modification callback works correctly
- Follow flag tests: Verify recursive crawling respects rule follow settings
- Depth tracking tests: Verify MaxCrawlDepth enforcement at various levels
- External link tests: Verify allow/deny external links works correctly
- Integration tests: Full spider crawling test site with multiple page types
- Concurrent crawling tests: Verify thread-safety with parallel rule evaluation
- LinkExtractor integration tests: Verify rules work with XPath/CSS restrictions
- Error handling tests: Verify graceful handling of invalid callback method names
- Performance tests: Measure rule matching overhead with 100+ rules
- Edge case tests: Empty rules, overlapping patterns, circular references, very deep crawling
- Real-world tests: Crawl production sites with complex navigation patterns

