## BACKGROUND & CONTEXT

Link extraction is the mechanism that transforms a web crawler from a single-page fetcher into a site-wide discovery engine. While conceptually simple ("find all links on a page"), production-grade link extraction involves sophisticated filtering, normalization, and deduplication to focus crawling on relevant content while avoiding traps like infinite URL spaces, calendar applications, and session-based URLs. Scrapy's LinkExtractor is one of its most widely-used components, providing a declarative way to control crawl scope through allow/deny patterns, domain restrictions, and content filters. For Ghost, implementing link extractors is essential because it eliminates boilerplate code that every spider would otherwise need to write manually. It enables focused crawling strategies where spiders start at a seed URL and automatically discover relevant pages without explicit URL lists. The extractor must handle malformed HTML gracefully, support both fast (Lxml-based) and flexible (Regex-based) parsing modes, and integrate seamlessly with the request generation pipeline.

## SCRAPY ANALYSIS

Scrapy's LinkExtractor architecture provides a clean separation between link discovery (parsing HTML) and link filtering (deciding which links to follow). The LxmlLinkExtractor uses the high-performance lxml library for HTML parsing, while SgmlLinkExtractor (deprecated) used SGMLParser. Key filtering capabilities include: allow/deny patterns (regex for URL matching), allow_domains/deny_domains (restrict by hostname), deny_extensions (skip .pdf, .jpg, etc.), restrict_xpaths/restrict_css (only extract links from specific page regions), tags/attrs (which elements to scan, default a/@href), canonicalize (normalize URLs), unique (deduplicate per page), and strip (clean whitespace). The extractor processes HTML content, extracts href attributes, resolves relative URLs against the response URL, applies all filters, and returns a list of Link objects. Links can be converted to Requests for the scheduler. The architecture supports custom processors for URL transformation before filtering.

## GHOST INTEGRATION

Ghost currently requires spiders to manually parse HTML and extract links using custom code. This feature integrates with: (1) NavigationConfiguration which will host LinkExtractorConfiguration; (2) the Spider base class which will provide ExtractLinksAsync helper method; (3) the Response class which will expose parsed HTML content via IHtmlDocument interface; (4) the Request factory for converting links to requests with proper metadata; and (5) the signal system for emitting link extraction events. The extractor will support dependency injection of custom filters and processors. Integration with the duplicate filter (Phase 1) will prevent extracting already-seen URLs, and integration with the storage system will support caching extracted links for debugging.

## TECHNICAL REQUIREMENTS

1. Create ILinkExtractor interface with ExtractLinks method
2. Implement LxmlLinkExtractor using AngleSharp or similar high-performance HTML parser
3. Implement RegexLinkExtractor for scenarios where regex pattern matching is preferred
4. Support allow/deny URL patterns using Regex with caching for performance
5. Support domain filtering (allow_domains, deny_domains lists)
6. Support file extension filtering (deny_extensions list like [.pdf, .jpg])
7. Support XPath/CSS restrictions to limit extraction to specific page regions
8. Support configurable tag/attribute pairs (default: a/@href, but also area/@href, etc.)
9. Implement URL canonicalization: resolve relative URLs, normalize encoding, remove fragments
10. Support unique filtering per page to avoid duplicate links in same extraction
11. Integration with Request factory to create properly configured requests from links
12. Support custom link processors for URL transformation before filtering

## CONFIGURATION DESIGN

```csharp
public sealed class LinkExtractorConfiguration
{
    public bool Enabled { get; set; } = true;
    public ExtractorType Type { get; set; } = ExtractorType.Lxml;
    public List<string> AllowPatterns { get; set; } = new();  // Regex patterns
    public List<string> DenyPatterns { get; set; } = new();
    public List<string> AllowDomains { get; set; } = new();
    public List<string> DenyDomains { get; set; } = new();
    public List<string> DenyExtensions { get; set; } = new() { ".pdf", ".jpg", ".png", ".gif", ".zip" };
    public List<string> RestrictXpaths { get; set; } = new();  // Extract only from these regions
    public List<string> RestrictCssSelectors { get; set; } = new();
    public List<TagAttributePair> Tags { get; set; } = new() { 
        new TagAttributePair { Tag = "a", Attribute = "href" }
    };
    public bool CanonicalizeUrls { get; set; } = true;
    public bool UniquePerPage { get; set; } = true;
    public bool StripWhitespace { get; set; } = true;
    public int MaxLinksPerPage { get; set; } = 0;  // 0 = unlimited
    public bool ProcessJavascriptLinks { get; set; } = false;  // Extract href="javascript:..."
}

public enum ExtractorType
{
    Lxml,   // Fast, standards-compliant HTML5 parsing
    Regex   // Flexible, pattern-based extraction
}

public sealed class TagAttributePair
{
    public required string Tag { get; set; }
    public required string Attribute { get; set; }
}

public interface ILinkExtractor
{
    IAsyncEnumerable<Link> ExtractLinksAsync(Response response, LinkExtractorConfiguration config, CancellationToken ct = default);
}

public sealed class Link
{
    public required string Url { get; set; }
    public string? Text { get; set; }
    public string? Fragment { get; set; }
    public int? Nofollow { get; set; }
    public string? Rel { get; set; }
    public Dictionary<string, string> Attributes { get; set; } = new();

    public Request ToRequest() => new() { Url = Url, Metadata = { ["link_text"] = Text } };
}

public sealed class LinkExtractionResult
{
    public List<Link> Links { get; set; } = new();
    public List<string> FilteredUrls { get; set; } = new();  // For debugging
    public TimeSpan ExtractionTime { get; set; }
    public int TotalElementsScanned { get; set; }
}
```

## IMPLEMENTATION NOTES

- Use AngleSharp library for LxmlLinkExtractor (modern, fast, HTML5-compliant parser for .NET)
- Compile regex patterns once and cache for performance
- For XPath/CSS restrictions, use AngleSharp's query capabilities to find root elements, then extract links only from those subtrees
- URL canonicalization: resolve relative URLs against response URL using System.Uri, normalize percent-encoding, remove fragments (optionally), sort query parameters for consistency
- Deny extensions check should be case-insensitive and handle URLs without extensions
- Domain filtering should support wildcards (*.example.com) using regex or proper domain matching
- Implement async streaming with IAsyncEnumerable to handle pages with thousands of links without buffering
- Support for base tag resolution (href in base tag changes relative URL resolution)
- Handle malformed HTML gracefully (AngleSharp does this automatically)
- Support for meta refresh tags as alternative link sources
- Support for canonical link tags to deduplicate pages with multiple URLs
- Implement link text extraction for better crawl context
- Support nofollow attribute detection for SEO-aware crawling
- Add extraction statistics for monitoring and debugging (time taken, links found, filtered)

## TESTING STRATEGY

- Unit tests: Verify link extraction from various HTML structures (simple, nested, malformed)
- Regex pattern tests: Verify allow/deny patterns match correctly with anchors and wildcards
- Domain filter tests: Verify allow_domains/deny_domains work with exact matches and wildcards
- Extension filter tests: Verify deny_extensions blocks specified file types
- XPath/CSS restriction tests: Verify extraction limited to specified page regions only
- URL canonicalization tests: Verify relative URLs resolved correctly, fragments handled per config
- Performance tests: Measure extraction time for pages with 10,000+ links
- Memory tests: Verify streaming extraction with IAsyncEnumerable uses constant memory
- Integration tests: End-to-end spider crawl using link extractor with real websites
- Real-world HTML tests: Test with various website types (WordPress, SPAs, legacy sites)
- Concurrent extraction tests: Verify thread-safety with multiple spiders extracting simultaneously
- Edge case tests: Empty pages, pages with no links, circular redirects, invalid URLs, encoding issues

