# Background & Context

HTTP redirects are the navigation backbone of the modern web, allowing content to move, consolidate, and reorganize while maintaining accessibility through seamless forwarding. For web crawlers, robust redirect handling is essential: without it, crawlers miss content when pages move (301/308 permanent redirects), get stuck in infinite loops when sites have redirect chains or circular references, and fail to handle complex scenarios like meta refresh tags, JavaScript redirects, and protocol upgrades (HTTP to HTTPS). Production crawlers must balance following redirects to reach target content with protecting against abuse through redirect loops, excessive chain lengths, and malicious redirect patterns. Scrapy's redirect middleware has been refined through years of production use, handling edge cases like cross-domain redirects, cookie preservation through redirects, and redirect method changes. Ghost needs equivalent capabilities to handle real-world websites where redirects are ubiquitous - login flows, URL shortening, mobile/desktop switching, and CDN edge routing all rely heavily on redirect mechanisms.

# Scrapy Analysis

Scrapy's redirect middleware (RedirectMiddleware) handles HTTP 3xx status codes and optionally meta refresh tags. Key features include: configurable maximum redirect depth (REDIRECT_MAX_TIMES, default 20) to prevent infinite loops, automatic following of 301, 302, 303, 307, and 308 status codes, handling of redirect chains where one redirect leads to another, loop detection by tracking visited URLs in the redirect chain, and cookie/session persistence through redirects. The middleware integrates with the downloader to intercept responses with redirect status codes, extract the Location header, create a new request to the target URL, and pass it back to the scheduler. Meta refresh handling (REDIRECT_ENABLED) parses HTML for meta tags like <meta http-equiv=\"refresh\" content=\"0;url=/new-path\">. Scrapy also supports priority adjustment for redirected requests and preserves request metadata through the redirect chain. The implementation tracks redirect depth in request metadata to enforce limits and detect loops.

# Ghost Integration

Ghost currently relies on HttpClient's built-in redirect handling for simple scenarios and Playwright's automatic navigation for browser automation. However, this lacks visibility and control over redirect behavior, making it impossible to detect loops, limit chain length, or track redirect paths for debugging. This middleware integrates with: (1) the Request/Response pipeline where it intercepts redirect responses before they reach the spider; (2) the Scheduler which receives follow-up requests for redirect targets; (3) the Spider base class which can access redirect chain metadata in responses; (4) the Configuration system for redirect policy setup; and (5) the signal system for emitting RedirectFollowed events. The middleware will support both automatic redirect following (like Scrapy) and manual redirect handling where spiders can inspect and decide on redirects. Integration with browser automation will track redirects that happen natively in Playwright.

# Technical Requirements

1. Create RedirectMiddleware implementing IMiddleware for redirect handling
2. Support HTTP 301, 302, 303, 307, 308 status code detection and handling
3. Implement maximum redirect hops configuration with loop detection
4. Support Location header parsing with relative and absolute URL resolution
5. Implement redirect chain tracking in request metadata for debugging
6. Support meta refresh tag parsing and handling (optional feature)
7. Handle redirect method changes (GET after 302/303, preserve method for 307/308)
8. Support cookie/session persistence through redirect chains
9. Integration with signal system to emit events for each redirect followed
10. Support for custom redirect policies via IRedirectPolicy interface
11. Implement redirect loop detection using hash set of visited URLs
12. Handle edge cases: missing Location header, malformed URLs, protocol-relative URLs

