# Background & Context

DNS resolution is often the forgotten bottleneck in high-performance web crawling. Every HTTP request begins with a DNS lookup to translate hostnames into IP addresses, and in a production crawling environment making thousands of requests per minute to the same domains, these lookups become a significant performance drain. Without DNS caching, crawlers repeatedly resolve the same hostnames, adding 50-500ms of latency per request and creating unnecessary load on DNS infrastructure. Browser automation frameworks like Playwright handle some caching internally, but HttpClient-based requests and direct socket connections benefit enormously from an application-level DNS cache. This middleware addresses the fundamental performance optimization that separates production-grade crawlers from proof-of-concept implementations. By caching DNS resolutions in memory with TTL-aware expiration, Ghost can achieve sub-millisecond hostname lookups for frequently-accessed domains, dramatically improving throughput while reducing dependency on external DNS services.

# Scrapy Analysis

Scrapy provides DNS caching through integration with Twisted's reactor, which caches DNS resolutions automatically when using the default DNS resolver. The cache is in-memory and respects TTL values from DNS records, ensuring that expired entries are refreshed appropriately. Key characteristics include: automatic caching with no configuration required, TTL-aware expiration that prevents stale entries, memory-bounded storage to prevent unbounded growth, and seamless integration with both HTTP and HTTPS requests. Scrapy also supports custom DNS resolvers through the DNS_RESOLVER setting, allowing users to swap the default implementation for alternatives like twisted.internet.asyncioreactor.AsyncioResolver. The caching layer operates transparently - spiders and middlewares are unaware of DNS caching, which happens at the network layer. This approach provides immediate performance benefits without code changes, but offers limited visibility and control over cache behavior, hit rates, and statistics.

# Ghost Integration

Ghost operates in a dual-mode environment where both HttpClient and Playwright browser automation are used for requests. While Playwright manages its own DNS caching internally through the browser engine, HttpClient requests benefit significantly from middleware-level DNS caching. This feature integrates with: (1) HttpClient configuration where DnsCacheMiddleware will intercept DNS resolution calls; (2) the Request pipeline where cached resolutions can be attached to requests for debugging and monitoring; (3) the Spider lifecycle where DNS cache statistics can be reported; and (4) the Configuration system for cache tuning. The middleware will support dependency injection of custom DNS resolvers and integrate with the signal system to emit cache events for analytics. Unlike Scrapy's transparent caching, Ghost's implementation will provide visibility into cache performance and allow fine-grained control over cache behavior.

# Technical Requirements

1. Create DnsCacheMiddleware class implementing IMiddleware for HttpClient integration
2. Implement thread-safe DNS cache storage using ConcurrentDictionary with expiration tracking
3. Support TTL-aware caching that respects DNS record TTL values from authoritative responses
4. Create IDnsResolver abstraction to allow pluggable resolver implementations
5. Implement DefaultDnsResolver using System.Net.Dns with async resolution
6. Support configurable maximum cache size with LRU eviction when limit exceeded
7. Implement cache pre-warming for seed domains to reduce cold-start latency
8. Support negative caching for failed resolutions to prevent repeated lookups
9. Integration with HttpClient's SocketsHttpHandler for DNS interception
10. Provide cache statistics: hit rate, miss rate, expired entries, memory usage
11. Support custom resolver injection for testing and custom DNS servers
12. Implement proper cleanup on disposal to prevent resource leaks

