## BACKGROUND & CONTEXT

Item loaders represent a declarative approach to data transformation in web scraping pipelines, providing a clean separation between raw extraction logic and data normalization concerns. In production scraping operations, extracted data rarely matches the desired output format directly—dates need parsing, strings require trimming, HTML entities must be decoded, and validation rules should enforce data quality. Without item loaders, this transformation logic gets scattered across spider code, creating maintenance nightmares and inconsistent data quality. Scrapy's ItemLoader has proven its value over years of production use, allowing developers to define input processors (transform raw data) and output processors (finalize before storage) through a fluent, chainable API. For Ghost, implementing item loaders is essential for building maintainable, production-grade spiders that produce consistent, high-quality data without repetitive boilerplate code. The loader pattern enables data scientists and engineers to focus on extraction logic while the framework handles the tedious but critical work of data normalization.

## SCRAPY ANALYSIS

Scrapy's ItemLoader architecture provides a powerful processor pipeline model that separates data extraction from transformation. Key concepts include: Input Processors that transform data as it enters the loader (e.g., MapCompose(str.strip, str.lower)), Output Processors that finalize data before retrieval (e.g., TakeFirst(), Join()), and nested loaders for extracting hierarchical data structures. The loader maintains an internal accumulator for each field, allowing multiple values to be collected and processed together. Default values ensure fields are never null, while field-specific processors override global defaults. The add_value(), add_xpath(), add_css(), and add_jmespath() methods provide multiple extraction strategies with automatic value accumulation. Processors are composable functions using MapCompose (apply functions sequentially to each value) and Compose (apply functions to the entire value list). Validation can be integrated at processor level with processors that throw exceptions for invalid data. The loader integrates seamlessly with Item objects and can be extended with custom processors for domain-specific transformations.

## GHOST INTEGRATION

This feature integrates with Ghost's Spider infrastructure at multiple points: (1) The Spider base class will provide CreateItemLoader() factory method for convenient loader instantiation; (2) EntityConfiguration will host ItemLoaderConfiguration defining default processors and validation rules; (3) The existing extraction strategies (XPath, CSS, JMESPath) will be extended to support loader-based extraction; (4) The Pipeline system will receive fully-processed items from loaders, eliminating the need for transformation pipelines; (5) Signal integration will emit events for loader creation, field population, and validation failures; (6) The SDK's fluent API will allow chainable loader operations matching Scrapy's ergonomics. Integration with the validation system will allow processors to throw structured exceptions that the spider can handle gracefully. The loader will support dependency injection for custom processors requiring external services.

## TECHNICAL REQUIREMENTS

1. Create IItemLoader interface with fluent API for adding values via XPath, CSS selectors, JMESPath, and direct values
2. Implement ItemLoader class with internal accumulator dictionary storing multiple values per field
3. Support Input Processors using MapCompose pattern (apply functions to each incoming value)
4. Support Output Processors using Compose pattern (apply functions to accumulated value list)
5. Implement built-in processors: TakeFirst, TakeLast, Join, Strip, LowerCase, UpperCase, RegexExtract, ParseDate, ParseInt, ParseDecimal, DefaultValue, ValidateNotNull, ValidateRegex
6. Support nested loaders for hierarchical data extraction (loader.GetNestedLoader() method)
7. Allow field-specific processor overrides via attributes or configuration
8. Support default values for fields to ensure non-null outputs
