## BACKGROUND & CONTEXT

Robots.txt compliance represents a fundamental ethical and legal consideration in web scraping operations. The Robots Exclusion Protocol (REP), commonly implemented via robots.txt files, allows website owners to specify which parts of their sites should not be crawled by automated agents. While not legally binding in most jurisdictions, respecting robots.txt demonstrates good faith, reduces risk of civil liability under computer fraud statutes, and maintains positive relationships with data sources. Many websites explicitly grant or deny scraping permissions through robots.txt directives, and some platforms use robots.txt as input to rate limiting and blocking decisions. Scrapy's built-in robots.txt middleware provides automatic compliance checking, caching, and user-agent matching. For Ghost, implementing robust robots.txt support is important for production deployments where compliance, reputation management, and risk mitigation are priorities. However, this middleware should be optional—aggressive scraping scenarios may need to bypass robots.txt restrictions, and the implementation must support both strict compliance and disabled modes.

## SCRAPY ANALYSIS

Scrapy's RobotsTxtMiddleware provides comprehensive robots.txt handling through the robotparser module. Key features include: Automatic fetching and parsing of robots.txt files for each domain encountered; User-agent matching using closest-match algorithm (specific bot rules, then * wildcard); Support for Allow and Disallow directives with path prefix matching; Crawl-delay directive parsing for rate limiting coordination; Sitemap directive extraction for discovery enhancement; Request-level override via dont_obey_robotstxt meta key; Configurable caching of parsed robots.txt data to avoid repeated fetches; and Integration with downloader middleware for automatic request filtering. The middleware fetches robots.txt at the domain root, parses directives using RFC-compliant rules, and filters outgoing requests against parsed rules before they reach the downloader. Support for crawl-delay allows coordination with AutoThrottle for respectful crawling speeds.

## GHOST INTEGRATION

This feature integrates as optional middleware in Ghost's request processing pipeline. Integration points include: (1) Middleware pipeline where RobotsTxtMiddleware implements ISpiderMiddleware for request filtering; (2) Configuration system with RobotsTxtConfiguration for enable/disable and caching options; (3) Cache service for storing parsed robots.txt rules with configurable TTL; (4) Downloader integration for fetching robots.txt files using existing HTTP infrastructure; (5) Spider base class with helper method IsUrlAllowedForCrawling(); (6) Signal system emitting RobotsTxtFetched, RobotsTxtParsed, and RequestBlockedByRobots events; (7) Scheduler integration preventing blocked URLs from entering the download queue; (8) Stats collection tracking robots.txt compliance metrics. The middleware will be disabled by default for aggressive scraping scenarios but easily enabled via configuration.

## TECHNICAL REQUIREMENTS

1. Create IRobotsTxtParser interface for parsing robots.txt content into structured rules
2. Implement RobotsTxtParser following Google Robots.txt Specification (de facto standard)
3. Support User-agent directive with closest-match selection algorithm
