## BACKGROUND & CONTEXT

The Request/Response Meta Dictionary is a fundamental infrastructure component that enables state management throughout the spider pipeline. In production web scraping, spiders rarely operate in isolation - they require passing contextual data between callbacks, tracking retry attempts, managing proxy rotations, maintaining session state, and coordinating with middleware components. Without a robust meta dictionary, developers are forced to use global state or awkward parameter passing, leading to unmaintainable code and race conditions in concurrent scenarios. This feature is essential because it provides the glue that holds together all other spider components: middlewares rely on meta to pass authentication tokens and proxy information, the retry middleware tracks attempt counts, the depth middleware tracks crawl depth, and custom extensions use meta for application-specific state. For Ghost to be a professional-grade framework, it must provide a thread-safe, type-safe, and serialization-capable meta dictionary that integrates seamlessly with all pipeline stages while maintaining performance under high concurrency.

## SCRAPY ANALYSIS

Scrapy's Request.meta dictionary is a simple Python dict that travels with the request through the entire scraping pipeline. It is initialized when the request is created, can be modified by spider callbacks, middlewares, and extensions, and is accessible when the response is received. Key built-in meta fields include: proxy (specifies proxy URL for this request), cookies (additional cookies to send), retry_count (number of retry attempts made), download_slot (controls concurrency slot assignment), depth (current crawl depth from start URL), callback (alternate callback method name), errback (error handler method name), priority (request priority override), dont_retry (skip retry middleware), handle_httpstatus_list (custom HTTP status handling), and many more. The meta dictionary is shallow-copied when requests are cloned (for retries or redirects), preserving the original state while allowing modifications. Scrapy's meta is not type-safe (Python is dynamically typed) but provides enormous flexibility for arbitrary data storage.

## GHOST INTEGRATION

The meta dictionary integrates with all major Ghost subsystems: (1) the Request/Response classes which will host the Meta property as a first-class citizen; (2) the Spider base class which provides typed accessor methods for common meta fields; (3) the Middleware pipeline where meta is read and modified by authentication, retry, proxy, and depth middlewares; (4) the SignalSystem which emits events when meta values change for observability; (5) the Scheduler which uses meta fields like priority and download_slot for request ordering; (6) the Storage system for persisting meta with requests for resumable crawls; and (7) the Configuration system for validating allowed meta keys and size limits. This is core infrastructure that must be implemented before most other middlewares and extensions can function correctly.

## TECHNICAL REQUIREMENTS

1. Create RequestMeta class as a thread-safe wrapper around Dictionary<string, object?> with concurrent access support
2. Implement deep cloning for meta when requests are duplicated (retries, redirects)
3. Support strongly-typed accessors for common meta fields (GetProxy(), SetRetryCount(), etc.)
4. Implement serialization support for JSON, MessagePack, and binary formats for persistence
5. Add size limits and key validation to prevent memory exhaustion from unbounded growth
6. Support metadata inheritance from parent requests to child requests in link extraction
7. Implement change tracking for debugging and audit logging
8. Provide async-safe APIs that work with CancellationToken in concurrent scenarios
9. Support custom meta validators registered via dependency injection
10. Implement meta compression for storage efficiency when persisting large crawls

