## BACKGROUND & CONTEXT

Scrapy's signal system represents one of the most elegant architectural patterns in web scraping frameworks. It provides an event-driven mechanism where different components (spiders, middlewares, extensions, pipelines) can subscribe to lifecycle events without tight coupling between publishers and subscribers. This publish-subscribe pattern is critical for Ghost because it enables true extensibility: third-party developers can build plugins, monitoring tools, and custom logic without modifying core framework code. The signal system allows for loose coupling where components react to state changes rather than directly calling each other, making the architecture more maintainable, testable, and scalable. For a professional-grade scraping framework like Ghost, signals are essential for production deployments where observability, metrics collection, error handling, and conditional logic based on spider state are non-negotiable requirements.

## SCRAPY ANALYSIS

Scrapy implements its signal system using a dispatcher pattern built on top of Twisted's deferreds, allowing for both synchronous and asynchronous handlers. The signal taxonomy is comprehensive and covers the entire scraping lifecycle: engine_started/engine_stopped for engine lifecycle management; spider_opened/spider_closed/spider_error for spider instance management; request_scheduled/request_dropped for request flow control; response_received/response_downloaded for response handling; and item_scraped/item_dropped/item_error/item_passed_through_pipeline for data processing pipeline. Signals are dispatched asynchronously by default, allowing multiple handlers to process events concurrently. Handlers receive typed event arguments containing contextual data and can modify data or trigger side effects. The system supports priority-based handler ordering and allows handlers to short-circuit signal propagation in certain cases.

## GHOST INTEGRATION

Ghost currently has a Spider lifecycle implemented through the Spider.cs class but lacks any mechanism for external components to react to lifecycle events. This feature integrates seamlessly with: (1) the existing Spider.RunAsync() method which will emit signals at key lifecycle points; (2) the Pipeline execution flow where item processing signals will be emitted before and after each pipeline stage; (3) the Request/Response handling layer in the messaging infrastructure; and (4) Storage abstractions for tracking spider state persistence. The signal system will be injectable through dependency injection, allowing services like the Scheduler, Downloader, and Pipeline to all participate in the event-driven architecture without direct references to each other.

## TECHNICAL REQUIREMENTS

1. Create SpiderSignal enum with all lifecycle events covering engine, spider, request, response, and item domains
2. Implement ISignalDispatcher interface with async dispatch capabilities supporting both sync and async handlers
3. Support subscription and unsubscription of handlers with proper cleanup to prevent memory leaks
4. Allow rich data passing through strongly-typed event args (SignalEventArgs hierarchy)
5. Thread-safe implementation using concurrent collections for scenarios with multiple spiders running in parallel
6. Integration with CancellationToken for graceful shutdown and cooperative cancellation of signal handlers
7. Support for handler priorities to control execution order
8. Error handling that prevents one failing handler from blocking others

