## BACKGROUND & CONTEXT

Sitemap-based crawling represents one of the most efficient and respectful approaches to large-scale web scraping. XML sitemaps, defined by the sitemaps.org protocol, provide machine-readable indexes of a website's content, offering significant advantages over traditional discovery methods: guaranteed completeness (all indexable URLs listed), freshness metadata (lastmod dates for change detection), priority hints (relative importance signals), and change frequency indicators (changefreq for update scheduling). For production scrapers targeting content-heavy sites (news, e-commerce, directories), sitemap crawling eliminates the inefficiency of following pagination, filters, and navigation hierarchies to discover deep content. Scrapy's SitemapSpider provides a specialized spider class that parses sitemap XML, handles sitemap index files (nested sitemaps), filters URLs based on patterns, and respects lastmod dates for incremental crawling. For Ghost, implementing sitemap spider support enables users to build highly efficient crawlers that start with comprehensive URL lists rather than crawling through site navigation, dramatically reducing server load and improving crawl completeness.

## SCRAPY ANALYSIS

Scrapy's SitemapSpider extends the base Spider class with sitemap-specific functionality. Key capabilities include: Parsing sitemap.xml and sitemap index files recursively; URL filtering using regex patterns (sitemap_rules) matching against sitemap loc entries; lastmod date parsing for filtering URLs by modification time; Priority and changefreq attribute extraction for scheduling hints; Support for compressed sitemaps (.gz extension); Automatic robots.txt sitemap discovery; Batch processing of sitemap URLs to control memory usage; and Integration with LinkExtractors for following additional links from parsed pages. The spider starts with a list of sitemap URLs, downloads and parses each sitemap (handling nested indexes), extracts URLs matching the rules, and yields requests for each matched URL. Users define sitemap_rules as tuples of (regex_pattern, callback_method) mapping URL patterns to parse handlers.

## GHOST INTEGRATION

This feature adds a new spider base class to Ghost's Spider hierarchy. Integration points include: (1) SitemapSpider base class inheriting from Spider with sitemap-specific functionality; (2) SitemapSpiderConfiguration for declarative sitemap URL lists and filtering rules; (3) XmlParser integration for robust sitemap XML parsing; (4) Scheduler integration for efficient enqueueing of discovered URLs; (5) DuplicateFilter integration preventing duplicate URLs from sitemap and page extraction; (6) Signal system emitting SitemapParsed, SitemapUrlDiscovered, and SitemapIndexParsed events; (7) LinkExtractor integration for optional link following from sitemap-discovered pages; (8) Stats collection tracking sitemap processing metrics. The implementation will support both simple sitemaps and complex sitemap index hierarchies commonly found on large sites.

## TECHNICAL REQUIREMENTS

1. Create SitemapSpider base class inheriting from Spider with sitemap crawling capabilities
2. Implement SitemapParser for parsing sitemap.xml and sitemapindex.xml formats
