## BACKGROUND & CONTEXT

Feed exports represent the final stage of the data extraction pipeline, transforming scraped items into persistent, shareable formats. Scrapy's feed export system is battle-tested in production environments handling billions of records, providing a flexible plugin architecture that supports multiple serialization formats, storage backends, and export configurations. For Ghost, implementing a robust feed export system is critical because it completes the data pipeline: spiders extract data, pipelines process and clean it, and feed exporters persist it in consumer-ready formats. Without this capability, Ghost would be an incomplete framework requiring users to implement their own export logic. Production scraping operations require automated exports for downstream analytics, machine learning pipelines, data warehouses, and regulatory compliance. The export system must handle large datasets efficiently through streaming, support incremental exports for resumable crawls, and provide configuration-driven flexibility for different use cases.

## SCRAPY ANALYSIS

Scrapy's feed exports architecture separates concerns between exporters (handle serialization), storage (handle persistence), and feed options (handle configuration). Out of the box, Scrapy supports: JSON Lines (newline-delimited JSON, ideal for streaming), JSON (array-based, human-readable), CSV (tabular, Excel-compatible), XML (structured, legacy system integration), Pickle (Python binary serialization), and Marshal (fast binary format). The architecture uses an ItemExporter base class that defines the interface, with concrete implementations handling format-specific serialization. Storage backends include local filesystem, FTP, S3, GCS, and Azure Blob. Configuration supports field filtering, ordering, encoding selection, and export item count limits. The system integrates with spider signals to automatically trigger exports on spider_close. Batch exports allow splitting large datasets into manageable chunks. Scrapy also supports post-processing hooks for compression and custom transformations.

## GHOST INTEGRATION

Ghost currently processes items through pipelines but lacks any mechanism to export results to external systems. This feature integrates with: (1) the existing PipelineConfiguration which will be extended with FeedExportConfiguration; (2) the Spider lifecycle where exports are triggered on spider completion or at configured intervals; (3) the Storage abstractions which will be extended with FeedStorage implementations for different backends; (4) the Item base class which provides the data model for serialization; and (5) the Configuration system for declarative export setup. The exporter system will be injectable and support both automatic export on spider_close and manual export via API calls. Integration with the signal system (Phase 1) will allow extensions to hook into export events for custom processing.

