## BACKGROUND & CONTEXT

Operational visibility is essential for production web scraping systems that run unattended for extended periods. Without proper monitoring and logging, operators cannot detect issues, track progress, or optimize performance until problems become critical. Periodic statistics logging provides a window into spider health, showing request rates, success rates, item throughput, error distributions, and resource utilization over time. Scrapy's LogStats extension demonstrates the value of periodic logging, emitting statistics at regular intervals for external log aggregation and monitoring systems. For Ghost, implementing comprehensive periodic stats logging is crucial for DevOps integration—logs feed into ELK stacks, Splunk, Datadog, and custom dashboards that track crawl health. JSON-formatted structured logs enable programmatic processing, alerting on thresholds, and trend analysis. This extension complements the StatsCollector by providing time-series visibility rather than just end-of-crawl summaries, enabling real-time operational awareness and proactive issue detection.

## SCRAPY ANALYSIS

Scrapy's LogStats extension provides interval-based statistics logging with configurable output formats. Key features include: Configurable logging interval (LOGSTATS_INTERVAL) controlling emission frequency; Structured statistics output showing requests, responses, items, and errors; Integration with StatsCollector for data aggregation; and Standard logging infrastructure integration. The extension uses a timer to periodically sample stats from the collector and emit log entries. Stats include counts (requests sent, responses received), rates (requests/minute), and errors (network failures, timeouts). The extension runs throughout the spider lifecycle, providing a continuous heartbeat of activity that operators can monitor.

## SCRAPY ANALYSIS - EXTENDED

Production scraping requires more sophisticated monitoring than basic count logging. Scrapy users typically extend LogStats with custom extensions that emit: Request latency histograms, Response status code distributions, Retry attempt counts, Item pipeline timing, Memory and CPU utilization, Queue depths, and Domain-specific statistics. These metrics feed into time-series databases (InfluxDB, Prometheus) and visualization tools (Grafana, Kibana). The extension architecture should support pluggable formatters (JSON, InfluxDB line protocol, Prometheus exposition format) and multiple output destinations (console, file, HTTP endpoint).

## GHOST INTEGRATION

This feature implements an Extension that periodically samples statistics and emits structured logs. Integration points include: (1) PeriodicStatsExtension implementing IExtension with timer-based execution; (2) StatsCollector integration for retrieving current statistics; (3) Signal system subscription for spider lifecycle events; (4) IStatsFormatter abstraction supporting multiple output formats; (5) IStatsOutput abstraction supporting multiple destinations; (6) Configuration system with PeriodicLogConfiguration for intervals and formats; (7) Logging infrastructure integration for console/file output; (8) Optional HTTP output for direct metrics API integration. The extension will support JSON, InfluxDB line protocol, and Prometheus formats out of the box.

