## BACKGROUND & CONTEXT

Duplicate request filtering is a foundational component of any production-grade web crawler. Without effective deduplication, crawlers waste resources re-fetching identical content, overwhelm target servers with redundant requests, and produce duplicate data that must be cleaned in post-processing. Scrapy's RFPDupeFilter (Request Fingerprint Dupe Filter) has been refined over a decade of production use, handling edge cases like URL canonicalization, query parameter normalization, and distributed crawling scenarios. For Ghost, implementing a robust duplicate filter is critical for scalability: as crawl sizes grow from thousands to millions of pages, the memory efficiency and lookup performance of the deduplication system become bottlenecks. The filter must support various strategies from simple in-memory sets to disk-backed storage to probabilistic bloom filters for web-scale crawling. Additionally, modern crawling requires handling dynamic URLs with session IDs, timestamps, and tracking parameters that must be normalized before deduplication.

## SCRAPY ANALYSIS

Scrapy's RFPDupeFilter uses request fingerprinting as its core mechanism. A fingerprint is a cryptographic hash (SHA1 or SHA256) of a canonicalized request representation that includes the HTTP method, URL (normalized), and body (for POST requests). The canonicalization process handles: URL case normalization (lowercase scheme and host), query parameter sorting (for consistent ordering), fragment removal (#anchors), and default port removal (e.g., :80 for HTTP). The filter maintains a Set<byte[]> of seen fingerprints in memory, with optional disk-backed storage for resumable crawls. Configuration options include: DUPEFILTER_CLASS to swap implementations, DUPEFILTER_DEBUG to log filtered URLs, and JOBDIR for persistent storage location. Scrapy also supports custom fingerprint algorithms through the request_fingerprint() function. For distributed crawling, Redis-based implementations allow shared deduplication across multiple spider instances. The filter integrates with the Scheduler, rejecting duplicate requests before they reach the downloader queue.

## GHOST INTEGRATION

Ghost currently has basic URL deduplication but lacks sophisticated fingerprinting and configuration options. This enhancement integrates with: (1) TargetConfiguration which will be extended with DupeFilterConfiguration; (2) the Scheduler service which queries the filter before enqueueing requests; (3) the Request class which will support fingerprint caching to avoid recomputation; (4) the Storage abstractions which will support persistent fingerprint storage; and (5) the CrawlContext which will expose filter statistics for monitoring. The enhanced filter will be injectable and support multiple backend implementations (memory, disk, Redis). Integration with the signal system will allow extensions to receive duplicate detection events for analytics and debugging.

## TECHNICAL REQUIREMENTS

1. Implement IRequestFingerprint interface for pluggable fingerprint algorithms
2. Create DefaultFingerprint using SHA256 hash of canonicalized request
3. Support URL canonicalization: lowercase scheme/host, sort query params, remove fragments
4. Support configurable query parameter filtering (ignore certain params like session IDs)
5. Implement IDupeFilter interface with SeenRequestAsync and Clear methods
6. Create MemoryDupeFilter using HashSet<byte[]> for in-memory deduplication
7. Create PersistentDupeFilter using embedded database for resumable crawls
8. Support TTL-based expiration for fingerprints to allow periodic re-crawling
9. Implement BloomFilterDupeFilter for probabilistic deduplication with configurable false-positive rate
10. Support maximum memory limits with LRU eviction for memory-constrained scenarios
11. Integration with Scheduler for pre-download filtering
12. Thread-safe implementation for concurrent request processing

