## Objective
Implement behavioral mimicry that makes browser automation indistinguishable from human users through realistic mouse movements, scroll patterns, and timing.

## Background
Advanced bot detection analyzes user behavior:
- Mouse movements (straight lines vs curves)
- Scroll patterns (smooth vs jerky)
- Timing (too fast vs human-like delays)
- Click locations (always center vs varied)

Real humans do not move mice in straight lines or click instantly.

## Acceptance Criteria
✅ Mouse movements follow Bezier curves (not straight lines)
✅ Variable movement speed (human variance)
✅ Realistic scroll behavior (acceleration/deceleration)
✅ Random delays between actions (2-5 seconds)
✅ Natural click locations (not always element center)
✅ Passes behavior analysis detection

## Implementation Details

### Bezier Curve Mouse Movement
```csharp
public class MouseMimicry
{
    public async Task MoveMouseHumanLikeAsync(IPage page, float targetX, float targetY)
    {
        var start = await page.Mouse.GetPositionAsync();
        var control = GetRandomControlPoint(start, targetX, targetY);

        // Bezier curve with 20-50 steps
        for (double t = 0; t <= 1; t += 0.02)
        {
            var (x, y) = CalculateBezierPoint(t, start, control, (targetX, targetY));
            await page.Mouse.MoveAsync(x, y);
            await Task.Delay(Random.Next(5, 20)); // Variable delay per step
        }
    }

    private (float x, float y) CalculateBezierPoint(double t, 
        (float x, float y) start, 
        (float x, float y) control, 
        (float x, float y) end)
    {
        // Quadratic Bezier: B(t) = (1-t)²P₀ + 2(1-t)tP₁ + t²P₂
        var x = (1-t)*(1-t)*start.x + 2*(1-t)*t*control.x + t*t*end.x;
        var y = (1-t)*(1-t)*start.y + 2*(1-t)*t*control.y + t*t*end.y;
        return (x, y);
    }
}
```

### Scroll Behavior
```csharp
public class ScrollMimicry
{
    public async Task ScrollHumanLikeAsync(IPage page, int deltaY)
    {
        var steps = Random.Next(10, 30);
        var stepSize = deltaY / steps;

        for (int i = 0; i < steps; i++)
        {
            // Variable speed: accelerate then decelerate
            var speedFactor = 1 + Math.Sin((Math.PI * i) / steps);
            await page.Mouse.WheelAsync(0, stepSize * speedFactor);
            await Task.Delay(Random.Next(20, 100));
        }
    }
}
