## BACKGROUND & CONTEXT

Spider Contracts represent a revolutionary approach to testing web crawlers that eliminates the need for full integration tests while providing confidence in extraction logic. In production scraping environments, running full crawls to test spiders is prohibitively expensive in terms of time, network resources, and target server load. Contracts allow developers to define expected behavior declaratively using sample URLs and expected output schemas, then automatically validate that spiders behave correctly against those contracts. This feature is critical for Ghost because it enables test-driven development of spiders, provides regression protection when websites change their structure, and serves as living documentation of what each spider is expected to extract. Contracts catch bugs early in the development cycle, before spiders are deployed to production, and provide a safety net when refactoring extraction logic. For a professional framework, contracts are essential for maintaining quality across large spider codebases and enabling continuous integration pipelines to validate spiders without external network dependencies.

## SCRAPY ANALYSIS

Scrapy's spider contracts are implemented using Python decorators (@url and @returns) on spider methods. The @url decorator specifies a sample URL to fetch, while @returns specifies the expected item type and field validation rules. When contracts are executed, Scrapy creates a minimal crawler environment, fetches the sample URL, runs the spider callback, and validates that the returned items match the contract specifications. Contracts support: field presence validation (ensuring expected fields exist), field type validation (matching expected data types), field count validation (min/max items returned), and custom validation functions for complex assertions. The contract system integrates with Scrapy's testing framework, allowing contracts to be run via command line (scrapy check) or as part of test suites. Contracts can be marked as pre-built to use cached responses instead of fetching live URLs, enabling offline testing. The system provides detailed failure reporting with diffs between expected and actual output.

## GHOST INTEGRATION

Spider Contracts integrate with multiple Ghost subsystems: (1) the Spider base class which will expose Contract attributes and validation hooks; (2) the SignalSystem which emits contract execution events for monitoring and reporting; (3) the Request/Response infrastructure for fetching sample URLs and processing responses; (4) the Item system for validating extracted data against contract schemas; (5) the Configuration system for controlling contract execution parameters and test environments; and (6) the Testing framework which provides contract runners for xUnit integration. Contracts will work alongside the existing spider lifecycle, allowing spiders to define contracts as part of their implementation while keeping contract code separate from production crawling logic. Integration with the middleware pipeline ensures contracts execute in an environment as close as possible to production while maintaining isolation.

## TECHNICAL REQUIREMENTS

1. Create SpiderContract attribute for decorating spider methods with URL and expected returns
2. Implement ContractRunner service that executes contracts in isolated test environment
3. Support field-level validation with type checking, presence validation, and custom predicates
4. Implement response caching for offline contract testing without network requests
5. Support multiple contract types: ItemContract, RequestContract, ErrorContract for different scenarios
6. Create ContractResult class with detailed pass/fail information and error messages
7. Implement batch contract execution with parallel processing for faster test runs
8. Support contract inheritance allowing base spider classes to define shared contracts
9. Integration with xUnit for running contracts as part of standard .NET test suites
10. Implement contract coverage reporting to identify spiders lacking test coverage

